rested in seeking open  the opening of all these materials we're  not sure we're not sure yet it certainly  will be open to the press yeah certainly  and this Sullivan can get you a copy of  the report yeah what kind of a lesson is  there to be learned about the Warren  Commission how how much damage did they  do  by the way they conducted their inquiry  feeding and fanning the flames of the  structure well you have to remember that  the Warren Commission did its work  during the height of the Cold War when  there was extreme reservations of public  disclosure of anything I think what our  work has done has demonstrated that the  Warren Commission worked very hard in a  very limited period of time to get at  the answers here but there was a lot of  information that was not shared with the  Warren Commission the CIA did not share  information the FBI did not share much  of its information about organized crime  again in the in the context of national  security so you have to look at what the  Warren Commission did in the context of  the times and the fact that they did not  have access to materials that they  should have had access to at the time go  ahead and that is commented upon in the  report yes actually we did but the the  thing about the Warren Commission it  seems to me is that they had a very  limited amount of time but they also  were operating in a period of if you  when you study the go back and read  about what Lyndon Johnson was concerned  about and in a period of time where  there was a kind of a frantic belief  that there was a very dangerous time  that that perhaps this had been done by  the Russians or that it was a very  dangerous so they they were very eager  to proceed quickly and they were very  eager not to stir up and stimulate any  more trouble than they could cope with  and so that's part of the problem but  the real problem was that the Warren  Commission hid their documents too as  did the House Select Committee  I hide their documents and the church  committee we thought had all the  documents out there they also put  documents away so that each time the  assassination was investigated the  investigating bodies tended to hide the  documents so nobody could see who had  said what and what had been done and  that I think has stimulated a great deal  of problems the the work of the Warren  Commission is a striking example of the  way in which the event of the  assassination was so enmeshed in a  matrix of the Cold War I think is very  important to understand that the  assassination and the investigative  efforts that followed it were so so much  involved in so many events that we have  now come to characterize as the Cold War  the fact that we were able to take  advantage of the end of the Cold War by  grace of the legislation and good timing  I think has contributed to our ability  to to get at more of this information  but you must see the event for what it  was and that was in the events the  context of the Cold War

