 records Review Board  over these past four years they have  been a remarkable staff they have been  asked to do an enormous job in a  relatively short period of time the job  of searching in every corner and every  file cabinet for records that may  possibly be relevant to the  assassination and I want to express my  thanks and the thanks to the board to  each and every one of them especially  our staff that remained with us to the  end and in my view they're truly the  a-team the people that worked long long  hours in the past two months to finish  up all of the work of the board and to  work on this very important final report  that we present today and it's  especially important I think and  significant that we return today to this  room the archivists reception room in  the National Archives from which we  began our work over four years ago with  our first meeting as a review board and  to that extent it provides some closure  to this effort and a chance to recognize  the National Archives and Records  Administration which  has truly done a great job throughout  this entire process they have been I  think the review board's ultimate  partner in this effort I want to  especially thank Steve Tilly who has  been with us from the beginning the  person who is in charge of the  collection at College Park he has been  exceptional and we're counting on his  good work to continue the effort to make  these materials available to the public  and to collect further records that we  expect will be coming in in the months  and hopefully years to come so thank you  Steve for your fine work  finally I want to thank the public that  has helped us throughout this effort and  that is very important we have tried  from day one as a review board to  communicate as much as we possibly could  with the American public to take their  ideas their theories their suggestions  and put them to work to try to find  records that people believe might have  existed so we thank the public for that  communication the ongoing communication  that we've had through the public  meetings through the hearings that we  have had and through the letters and  calls that we've received literally  daily throughout this entire effort  thank you that has been an important  part of this effort and public  participation in this event that is long  overdue I'd like to ask dr. Lewis  Bilardo who is the deputy archivist of  the United States to come forward right  now I'd like to present dr. Blair doe  with a copy of the final report of the  assassination records Review Board in  the hope that the National Archives will  continue its important stewardship of  these records and to make them freely  available to the public not only today  and tomorrow but for generations to come  thank you dr. Blair  thank you judge tunheim and members and  staff of the review board I do want to  say that as you had indicated this was  an enormous job that this board and  staff undertook we received this report  with with great gratitude do want to say  that a remarkable body of records have  come to the National Archives because of  this work both government records as  well as some private materials as well  and not only are is an enormous amount  of documentation now exists about the  events if November 1963 in Dallas but  interestingly enough a large body of  material shedding light on other aspects  of government policy that we would never  have thought of when this whole thing  began for example American policy  relationship to Cuba the American policy  early in the early 1960s as it relates  to Vietnam and last but not least also  the investigations of the Justice  Department and the FBI as it relates to  organized crime so again thank you very  much for this and for the records that  have come our way take care  I'd like to ask other review board  members to speak now and present some of  their thoughts about this great event  I'm Henry Graff professor emeritus at  Columbia University in the history  department I would like to make a very  few and very brief comments underscoring  some of the points that Judge tunheim  offered we have to see that this board  had as its prime goal  the restoration insofar as we could  participate in the process of faith of  the people in the government of the  United States and we'd like to believe  that this above all the documents is our  major contribution and I'd like to add  also that just as the assassination is a  significant event in the history of the  Cold War the various theories the doubts  among the American people about what  actually happened on that awful day in  Dallas all of that is part also of  history a history that includes the  Vietnam War and includes Watergate goes  back perhaps to the Bay of Pigs  more recently it involves the medical  consequences of certain drugs and  perhaps whatever else even including  Agent Orange material that affected  gravely the lives of soldiers all of  this contributed to a terrible sense in  the public that the government was  hiding things and what our board was  created to produce is as President Bush  said in the message that he issued at  the time of the signing of the  legislation creating the JFK a  collection Act the aim primarily was to  restore the confidence  in the American people that the  government doesn't lie we don't know  that we have answered all the questions  that will arise that have arisen but  we'd like to think we've made a major  contribution in that regard a smattering  of applause Henry I'm Herman Hall Dean  of the College of Humanities at Ohio  State University the Ohio State  University and I'm also a professor of  history in the law I first want to thank  Jack tunheim for his leadership on the  board this was a really complex group of  people I think to work with jack has  shown great skill and keeping us  together and moving us toward our  objective Benjamin Franklin once said  that for three to keep a secret two must  be dead and if there is any testimony to  make about the work of the board that  even with all three dead it's possible  for the government to still want to keep  things under wraps if there is anything  that stands out about our work it is  that the Shroud of the Cold War still  rests over us that we can have documents  that are more in some cases more than 40  years old that the various government  agencies sought to protect is I think a  sad commentary on where we are with  openness nonetheless the board with  extraordinary powers an extraordinary  mandate tremendous sympathy I think on  the part of the community researchers  who have been out there working on the  assassination as I think put a profound  punctuation mark on the cause of  openness I went to however remind all of  you who are in this room all of you who  work in the archives all of you who  represent the agencies that the work of  the assassination records Review Board  while the board itself has finished is  far from done for it is important to  remember that there are documents yet to  be released their documents that are  redacted that will open between now the  year 2017  and without your conscious involvement  and continuing oversight and continuing  awareness that these documents deserve  to be open there are I think within the  government forces that will push just  the opposite direction so the challenge  I think for all of us is to remember  that the boards work the five of us are  finished but the spirit and the legacy  of the act go on and it's up to all of  you to make sure that government does  the right thing and that is to ensure  that that the lessons of secrecy are in  fact learned it's I think important to  remember as well that the legacy of this  board is hardly written today there are  approaching five million documents out  there and there will be more that will  be added to the collection over the year  it will be a decade I would submit to  you before we know what the real impact  of these documents are until scholars  and researchers have the opportunity to  work their way through them and in  working their way through them come to  understand they're really tremendous  significance of of what I believe we've  we've done earth so whatever story you  write whatever message you send it needs  to be a message that the story remains  to be completed both of the oversight of  the process of making sure that that  which we declare to be open is open that  for scholars and researchers to come to  rights of story I think in conclusion  that we all recognize that Justice  Robert Jackson was absolutely right when  he said that the Constitution is not a  suicide pact and by that Jackson meant  there are some secrets that are clearly  worth retaining and keeping because  they're in the public interest but after  four years and more than four million  documents surely we can all agree that  the numbers of those secrets are fewer  than one would have believed and more  profoundly one of the greatest threats  to our Liberty is the continuing  insistence that we we have more rather  than fewer secrets thank you  I'm Anna Nelson and I teach in the  history department at American  University as a person who has done  research and has taught American foreign  policy in the post-war period I was  always standing on the other side of the  barricades of secrecy always filing in  Freedom of Information requests and  harassing the National Archives for  documents and I think that it gave me a  very interesting perspective on what was  behind those barricades it wasn't always  something that I thought it was or  anyone else thought it was it has been  pointed out we soon came to discover  that many other records had been  released 20 years ago I think that there  were several unique things about what we  did one of the things is simply that  five of us came together from five  different communities you might say even  though we may share professional  interests we were all engaged in other  activities and without fail we agreed on  what to open five American citizens can  simply determine that after 35 years  there are some things too close to  protect people and there are most things  too open and we had a lot of learning to  do as a group but it was a very  interesting experience if we differed it  was often because one of us wanted to  open more it was almost never because we  decided to open less the second thing I  think that's important is that we have  through our work reached to audiences  one of course the one we were set out to  help were those people who have worked  on the  assassination done research on it  studied it for so many years and had  lost his confidence in what they were  finding as the documents eat doubt one  by one half blacked out on a page but  the other group that we have also helped  our researchers in every field and in  the long run American citizens because  we have opened so many documents from  the early 60s and we have learned the  public will learn a great deal about as  was mentioned before a great deal about  what in fact was the situation of the  government the government agencies and  very formative years in the early 1960s  begin to unfold because we had the  statute we had there are some documents  that came out automatically simply  because of the various agencies and the  presidential libraries knew that we were  in fact in business so I think we have  done our work well I think that I all of  us would agree with each other I think  at this point with some of the salient  features but I think that to me the  uniqueness and the product those  marvelous documents in the National  Archives will in fact remain our legacy  my name is Bill Joyce and my day job so  to speak is at the Princeton University  Library I'd like to say a few things  this morning and I think I'd like to  start by thanking or chairman our  colleague Jack tunheim for his very  skillful leadership first and bringing  the five of us together then assembling  a staff and finally working with the  public with the various government  agencies to move the help of excuse me  move the work of the board forward so  that we might begin to accomplish some  of what we set out to do second I think  I'd like to pay tribute to the staff a  more dedicated and loyal group of people  I can't imagine  we are very blessed to have had their  good work over the years and to have  been so sensual in in some respects and  guiding us as board members in terms of  what we've been able to accomplish they  deserve our heartiest thanks and  appreciation for the good effort that  they have put into this project Anna  said that the legacy of the board is in  the collection that a compilation of  four plus million pages of material at  College Park in the archives building  out there that is certainly the case and  as my colleague Kermit Hall has pointed  out the material that is there awaits  the attention of the historians the  journalists the researches to probe into  that material it is an extraordinarily  deep slice of information into the  workings of the government at a  tumultuous time in the history of the  Republic and I believe that our legacy  in terms of our contribution to  historical knowledge can scarcely be  plumb now but over the course of time  will become apparent to all of us but I  believe there is a second and equally  important legacy that we have created  and that is the process the model that  we have been a  to put into place using the legislation  the powers that were conferred upon us  to create a process by which we could  declassify federal records it is  important for all of us to recognize the  steps that we were able to take to work  to open increasing numbers of documents  some of them very sensitive indeed and  they believe that the procedures that we  were able to adopt have an impact and  have an importance that we must all pay  attention to  Kermit hall called attention to the fact  that it is responsibility of all of us  now that the board goes out of business  to remember that the work of opening  these records continues and I think in  that spirit we must all remember that it  is our responsibility to keep after this  to try to counter forces that do not  want these records to be opened and to  continue to work to accomplish as much  as we possibly can the openness not only  concerning the assassination but all  aspects of public life that is after all  our responsibilities as citizens thank  you  thank you very much to my fellow board  members and let me just add in closing  my thanks to all of them for being a  truly superb group of individuals very  talented and intelligent in their own  right and I think the collective  experience of this board has been an  outstanding piece of work and I want to  salute them for for that that effort  there's a couple of people that I want  to point out that I've seen here this  morning that just among a group of many  people who should be mentioned I already  mentioned Steve Tilly and he's important  but the person who served as our last  executive director of the review board  over the past several months which has  been a difficult time wrapping  everything up Laura dank Laura would you  wave wherever you are right in the back  and a bit earlier I saw and I'm not sure  if he's still here our first executive  director who really set up this entire  process dr. David Marr well there he has  David wave for a moment they were two of  the leaders of a very very fine staff a  group of as they've been called loyal  and dedicated employees and they  certainly have been and we've enjoyed  every minute of working with them now  the board will take a few questions if  you have questions to ask why don't I  get everyone up here so that we can all  participate in this way we need to stand  up here so  Bob go ahead before your arrival here  this morning one of the conspiracy  authors held forth it late at the links  telling us that's your investigation of  the autopsy findings of the medical  findings  proved what he and other conspiracy  theorists have said all along that there  was a conspiracy to distort the autopsy  and hide that what had been a a  collective conspiracy is that the view  of any or all of you or is that a trap  you fell into why recreating the autopsy  and casting doubt on its findings let me  answer that Bob by saying that we  attempted with the autopsy report to try  to clarify some of the many questions  that have been raised throughout the  years to the extent that clarifying  could be done now thirty five years  after the event we did our best to do  that I think we have shed greater light  on the autopsy findings which of course  have been among the most secretive  findings throughout the years and so I  think that we didn't attempt to reach  any particular conclusion about it we  tried to answer questions about what  additional records might be available  again to prepare those records for the  American public to make up their own  minds about it breathe at the end  results of your finding is not to  reassure the public and to mute their  suspicions about the assassination but  to add fresh doubts that will increase  the suspicions and I know you didn't  intend this but would you agree that is  the result you're speaking of our entire  effort now it's time  that but let me just say that we took  this investigation of the records which  is what this has been not an  investigation of the assassination  itself but a hunt and search for the  records to every possible place we could  take it  we didn't prejudge any of the results  and we don't really prejudge the answers  either the answers are really to be left  to the researchers to the writers who  will have the time and effort ability to  go through these records over a length  of time and reach their own conclusions  about what happened I'm convinced that  there will be many conclusions reached  from that because that's the nature of  this particular episode of American  history we're too far beyond the event  right now to have the kind of conclusive  answers that the American public would  like to see and so the answers will lie  in the work of the researchers and  interpreters in the years to come well  with the State Department we had no  problems with them and their release of  their own records in fact that process  moved very smoothly and they were very  dedicated to the law a portion of the  law instructed the Department of State  to assist us in trying to obtain release  of KGB records in Moscow and in Minsk  the records that through our  investigation we know exists in fact  we've seen many of them we've not been  able to obtain all what we would have  liked to have obtained and in part where  our criticism of the State Department is  that they really didn't spend enough  time helping us with that effort as far  as the president's foreign intelligence  board we worked with them late in the  process and and felt that they believed  that they had an exemption from this act  which is not apparent from the face of  the Act I think in the end their records  will be released like everyone else but  it was  little difficult task working with them  at the end check could I do something  here too I think that that both of these  matters are really of some moment we  know that materials are in the former  Soviet Union Jack and I Jack and I and  David Marr well certainly had the  opportunity to see them and Vince it's  just very unfortunate recognizing that  the relationship with the government of  Belarus is not the best still very  unfortunate that we couldn't bring more  effective diplomatic initiative to  support our effort to work there and  much the same I think with with with  Russia where we have every reason to  believe that there are additional  records relating to especially Oswald  and in terms of the president's foreign  intelligence advisory board we all need  to keep in mind that there's a learning  process and unfortunately they didn't  get their schooling early enough and the  result of this is that I think they hold  to some views about their authority  relative to the authority of the Act  that are not I think very progressive  and certainly are not going to be very  hopeful if they aren't forthcoming with  their records in really amplifying the  context in which the assassination took  took place so I need your questions  right on target and it's just  regrettable that we didn't get a little  bit more activity out of these two well  their view of their authority to be  independent of the scope of our of our  act and you know they they take I think  the position although you'd have to talk  with them they take the position that  they're they're essentially a private  advisory group to the to the to the  president and that they can't render a  good judgment unless they know that  everything they say is to be maintained  and absolute secrecy well I would submit  to you that is no way to run a democracy  and it's certainly no way to preserve  Liberty in the face of secrecy and  there's every reason to believe that  they're covered as comprehensively as we  are as all the other agencies are by by  the end  part of this to answer your question as  a matter of timing we had we did have  delay we actually had a sunset provision  of three years we had it extended for  four years largely because we could not  complete the CIA records we did not want  to go out of business unless we had  completed those records so delay is a  wonderful way to avoid coming to grips  with what was this necessary and the  same was true with the foreign records  there was a window of opportunity with  Belarus which we lost because of the  State Department losing a cable or not  finding the table and so that was this  kind of delay that I mean the remarkable  thing is we really thought we'd be  through processing records in early  August records were processed over this  last weekend we still have two  outstanding issues before we go out of  business tomorrow so timing has been  very critical to what we've done all the  way along the road yes I would like to  respond a little further to Bob Clark  who has been an old supporter I think of  our process and with whom we've had many  contacts the board does not subscribe to  any conspiracy theory it was not  intending to create a new conspiracy  theory in getting at the autopsy  photographs our mission has not been to  redo the Warren Commission report our  mission was to open documents and I know  the law of unintended consequences I  think that was what Bob had in mind when  he wondered what will happen to the  product to the legacy of this board we  don't know our intentions were however  not to create new conspiracy theories  because that was not what we were organ  nice appointed by the president  confirmed by the Congress to do part of  our work was to seek out records  wherever they existed whether they were  in local government hands state  government hands or private hands as  well we encouraged donations of records  and films and other artifacts to this  collection and we were really blessed by  an enormous amount of donated material  which now is available to the American  public one of the most important aspects  of this I think are the records from the  New Orleans chapter of this event the  prosecution in 1967 of Clay Shaw by  District Attorney Jim Garrison virtually  all the records that remain from that  event have been donated to the  collection including the long secret  grand jury testimony from that  proceeding we faced a few obstacles in  New Orleans but by and large we got  great cooperation there we got good  cooperation in Dallas as well especially  with the donation of additional films  which will enrich this collection could  you just take off some of the new  material that you are some of the things  we haven't seen before well we're not  technically releasing new material today  because we've been working on this  effort for four years and we have been  releasing material as it has become  available but I think generally if you  look at the collection some of the most  important documents include the  documents about Cuba and Operation  Mongoose which was in operation in the  early 1960s to try to destabilize and  perhaps even assassinate Castro those  records are very  significant they were caught up in the  sweep of these records because of our  broad definition of an assassination  record also I think of significant  interest are all of the records of the  mysterious visit that Lee Harvey Oswald  allegedly made in Mexico City in late  September early October of 1963 in which  he sought passage through Cuba to the  back to the Soviet Union the  investigative reports on that visit have  now been made public through the work of  the review board with very little  redactions and I think that's a very  significant collection as well should be  clarified because you didn't intend to  say that you're taking a no conspiracy  position your position is that you're  not taking a position on whether there  was a conspiracy or there wasn't but  your staff did conduct one of the most  important inquiries in this country's  history in that it re-interviewed the  autopsy doctors over the last two years  and the photographic personnel in the  weight of that evidence tends to  indicate that the evidence in the  National Archives is false but you don't  have to comment on that that's not part  of your report we accept the invitation  it's certainly not the case so we  anticipate presenting our report  tomorrow to the president there has been  no embargo on any aspect of the report  it's it's there's no part of it that's  being held up or anything we're just  we're just going to present the report  to the president tomorrow who has been  very interested in our work and remains  very interested in seeking open  the opening of all these materials we're  not sure we're not sure yet it certainly  will be open to the press yeah certainly  and this Sullivan can get you a copy of  the report yeah what kind of a lesson is  there to be learned about the Warren  Commission how how much damage did they  do  by the way they conducted their inquiry  feeding and fanning the flames of the  structure well you have to remember that  the Warren Commission did its work  during the height of the Cold War when  there was extreme reservations of public  disclosure of anything I think what our  work has done has demonstrated that the  Warren Commission worked very hard in a  very limited period of time to get at  the answers here but there was a lot of  information that was not shared with the  Warren Commission the CIA did not share  information the FBI did not share much  of its information about organized crime  again in the in the context of national  security so you have to look at what the  Warren Commission did in the context of  the times and the fact that they did not  have access to materials that they  should have had access to at the time go  ahead and that is commented upon in the  report yes actually we did but the the  thing about the Warren Commission it  seems to me is that they had a very  limited amount of time but they also  were operating in a period of if you  when you study the go back and read  about what Lyndon Johnson was concerned  about and in a period of time where  there was a kind of a frantic belief  that there was a very dangerous time  that that perhaps this had been done by  the Russians or that it was a very  dangerous so they they were very eager  to proceed quickly and they were very  eager not to stir up and stimulate any  more trouble than they could cope with  and so that's part of the problem but  the real problem was that the Warren  Commission hid their documents too as  did the House Select Committee  I hide their documents and the church  committee we thought had all the  documents out there they also put  documents away so that each time the  assassination was investigated the  investigating bodies tended to hide the  documents so nobody could see who had  said what and what had been done and  that I think has stimulated a great deal  of problems the the work of the Warren  Commission is a striking example of the  way in which the event of the  assassination was so enmeshed in a  matrix of the Cold War I think is very  important to understand that the  assassination and the investigative  efforts that followed it were so so much  involved in so many events that we have  now come to characterize as the Cold War  the fact that we were able to take  advantage of the end of the Cold War by  grace of the legislation and good timing  I think has contributed to our ability  to to get at more of this information  but you must see the event for what it  was and that was in the events the  context of the Cold War
 

